# Project Goals & Roadmap

**Vision**: Build a **mostly faithful** (pretty close, gameplay-accurate) highly modifiable C implementation of Super Metroid. Prioritize development velocity and moddability over strict 100% pixel-perfect matching. Evolve from hybrid SNES emulation into a clean, data-driven "canvas" engine. Small memory/OAM/timer diffs are acceptable if core behavior matches. Every aspect (physics, entities, levels, AI) must be editable at runtime or via data files. Support modding for physics, adding players, multiplayer, new enemies, custom worlds, stories, battle arenas, and ROM-hacks. Work in small, testable, iterable baby steps. Hybrid C/assembly is explicitly allowed.

## Architecture Target
- **Emulation Core**: Keep PPU/sound accuracy; use as fallback.
- **Game Logic**: All original code ported to clean C. Exposed via headers.
- **Data-Driven**: Physics, enemy stats, level data, palettes in editable tables/files (JSON/C arrays with hot-reload).
- **Entity System**: Modular for Samus (multi-instance), enemies, projectiles, tiles. Easy extension.
- **Mod Canvas**: Live-editing overlay (ImGui?), script hooks (Lua?), "vibe edit" any param/behavior.
- **Hooks**: Pre/post function patches, override tables.
- **Output**: Play original, export ROM-hacks, standalone custom campaigns, RL environments.

Hybrid mode: C implementations take precedence; unported sections dispatch to original assembly/ROM via CPU infra.

